NNAAMMEE
       roffsub - a roff subset for manuals read in a terminal

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       A roffsub document is a man page. Every roff program displays it: man,
       nroff, groff, mandoc. It is also simple enough for small awk and C
       routines to render from inside a program's own binary or bash function,
       at the width of the terminal in use, with bold and italic where the
       terminal can show them. One source then serves as the installed page and
       as the program's built-in help.

       This page defines the source form, how it is rendered, and how a program
       presents it. The form is checked; content is not.

EELLEEMMEENNTTSS
        EELLEEMMEENNTT         WWRRIITTTTEENN AASS                  SSHHOOWWNN AASS
       ---------------+---------------------------+-----------------------------
        title           .TH                         header and footer, optional
                                                    in help
        section         .SH                         bold heading at the margin
        subsection      .SS                         bold heading at half an
                                                    indent
        paragraph       .PP .P .LP                  filled text
        definition      .TP, tag on the next line   tag, body hanging at the
                                                    indent
        list item       .IP                         as a definition, tag inline
        nested block    .RS .RE                     margin moved in and back
        literal block   .nf .fi                     lines as written
        emphasis        font escapes and requests   bold literals, italic
                                                    arguments
        spacing         .sp .br                     blank lines, a line break
        table           .TS .TE                     open columns, horizontal
                                                    rules
        comment         .\"                         nothing

SSOOUURRCCEE FFOORRMM
       Each rule states its purpose, so it can be judged by whether it achieves
       it.

   LLeexxiiccaall rruulleess
       o  Bytes 0x20 to 0x7E and newline only. A byte is then a column, so awk
          and C measure width with a plain length.

       o  No tab characters. Layout is stated by requests, so every viewer shows
          the same thing, and tab is free to separate fields in the compiled
          form.

       o  Line 1 is ''\\"" tt when, and only when, the page has a table, so that man
          runs tbl first. It is the only line that begins with an apostrophe.

       o  A control line is a period followed directly by a request listed
          below. A comment is a whole line beginning with a period, a backslash,
          and a double quote.

       o  In fill mode a text line begins with a word: never a space, never
          empty, and never a period or apostrophe unless written after \&. roff
          treats a leading space or a blank line as a layout instruction;
          stating breaks with requests gives them one meaning everywhere.

       o  One sentence per line is recommended. Diffs stay small and sentence
          spacing stays predictable.

   RReeqquueessttss
       Widths are a bare number or a number followed by n, counting character
       cells.

        RREEQQUUEESSTT                 AARRGGUUMMEENNTTSS                MMEEAANNIINNGG
       -----------------------+------------------------+------------------------
        .TH                     NAME SEC DATE SOURCE     page title; first
                                MANUAL                   request; @DATE@ and
                                                         @VERSION@ are filled
                                                         in at install and
                                                         compile
        .SH .SS                 heading                  section, subsection
        .PP .P .LP              none                     new paragraph; resets
                                                         the prevailing indent
        .TP                     [width]                  tagged paragraph; the
                                                         next line, text or a
                                                         font request, is the
                                                         tag
        .IP                     [tag [width]]            indented paragraph,
                                                         optionally tagged
        .RS                     [width]                  move the margin in by
                                                         width or by the
                                                         prevailing indent
        .RE                     none                     restore the margin
        .nf .fi                 none                     literal and fill mode;
                                                         literal blocks hold
                                                         text only
        .B .I                   words                    bold or italic words
        .BR .RB .IR .RI .BI     words                    two fonts alternating
        .IB                                              word by word
        .sp                     [N]                      N blank lines, default
                                                         1
        .br                     none                     line break
        .TS .TE                 none                     table

       Double quotes group words into one argument; a quote inside is written
       \(dq. A request takes at most 9 arguments.

   EEssccaappeess
        EESSCCAAPPEE        MMEEAANNIINNGG                          TTEERRMMIINNAALL OOUUTTPPUUTT
       -------------+--------------------------------+-----------------
        \fB \fI \fR   bold, italic, roman              font change
        \fP           previous font                    font change
        \-            minus, copies as a real hyphen   -
        \e            backslash                        \
        \&            zero-width guard                 nothing
        \(space)      space that never breaks          space
        \(em          em dash                          --
        \(en          en dash                          -
        \(bu          bullet                           o
        \(aq          apostrophe                       '
        \(dq          double quote                     "

       The font returns to roman by the end of every request line and table
       cell, so emphasis never runs past where it was meant to stop.

   TTaabblleess
       .TS
       box tab(@);
       lb lb
       l  l.
       OPTION@MEANING
       _
       \fB\-r\fR@reverse the order
       .TE

       The options line is required and ends with a semicolon. It names the cell
       separator with tab(c), a printable character that is not a letter or
       digit and appears in no cell, and may add box, allbox, and center. Format
       lines follow, the last ending with a period; keys are l, r, and c, with
       modifiers b, i, and w(N), a minimum width. Format line i governs data row
       i and the last repeats. Each data row has one cell per column; a row of a
       single underscore is a rule. A cell opened with T{ continues on the
       following lines until a line beginning T}, and is filled within its
       column. roff wraps only these text blocks, so any cell that may need
       wrapping is written as one; the roffsub renderers wrap every cell. The
       page installed for man is written by roffsub -n, which rewrites each
       table so that roff draws it the same way: an empty separator column
       between the columns, rules joined by a + in those columns, no vertical
       lines, and columns holding text blocks widened to fill the line.

   CCoonntteenntt
       A conventional order is NAME as _n_a_m_e \- _s_u_m_m_a_r_y, then SYNOPSIS,
       DESCRIPTION, OPTIONS, EXAMPLES, ENVIRONMENT, FILES, EXIT STATUS, SEE
       ALSO. The order is guidance; content belongs to the author.

RREENNDDEERRIINNGG

   WWiiddtthh aanndd mmaarrggiinnss
       Text fills to the width with a right margin of 0, or to the measure when
       one is set. Words are packed greedily with a ragged right edge; a word
       too long for its line runs past the margin whole. Literal lines are never
       rewrapped. The indent unit is 7 cells, or 4 when the width is under 60.

   IInnddeennttaattiioonn aanndd ssppaacciinngg
       o  A section heading sits at the margin and its body one unit in; a
          subsection heading sits half a unit in.

       o  Each paragraph, tagged paragraph, and indented paragraph is preceded
          by one blank line, except directly after a heading.

       o  A tag shorter than the prevailing indent shares its line with the
          body; a tag as long or longer puts the body on the next line.

       o  A width given to .TP or .IP becomes the prevailing indent for later
          items until the next .SH, .SS, or .PP.

       o  .RS saves the margin and prevailing indent, moves the margin in, and
          resets the prevailing indent; .RE restores both.

   TTaabblleess
       Tables draw no vertical lines. Each cell has one space of padding on each
       side and columns meet at one separator position, a space in data rows and
       a plus sign in rules, so column text sits three cells apart. Rules appear
       where the source has an underscore row; box adds one above and below,
       allbox one after every row.

       When a table is wider than the space available, the widest columns give
       up width first, down to a common cap, never below their longest word, and
       short columns keep their natural width. Cells wrap within their columns.
       When even the longest words do not fit, each row is shown as lines of the
       form _h_e_a_d_e_r: _v_a_l_u_e.

   EEmmpphhaassiiss
        SSTTYYLLEE        UUSSEEDD FFOORR                    BBOOLLDD     IITTAALLIICC
       ------------+---------------------------+--------+-------------------
        sgr          a terminal                  ESC[1m   ESC[4m, underline
        overstrike   a pager                     c BS c   _ BS c
        plain        a file, a pipe, TERM=dumb   none     none
        pre          pre-formatting              \fB      \fI

       Control bytes are added only as a line is written, so they never count
       toward its width. Emphasis is closed before every newline and reopened on
       the next line. The SGR parameters may be replaced, so that bold and
       italic show as colors.

PPRROOGGRRAAMM IINNTTEERRFFAACCEE

   HHeellpp
       ----hheellpp presents the full manual. What it writes is fixed by four inputs:
       the embedded manual, the width, the style, and the destination. The C and
       bash forms of a program write the same bytes for the same inputs.

        OOUUTTPPUUTT                   GGOOEESS TTOO            SSTTYYLLEE        WWIIDDTTHH
       ------------------------+------------------+------------+----------
        terminal                 the pager          overstrike   terminal
        terminal, PAGER is cat   the terminal       sgr          terminal
        file or pipe             the file or pipe   plain        80

       The pager is PAGER when set, otherwise less, otherwise more. The width is
       MANWIDTH when set, otherwise COLUMNS, otherwise the terminal's own.
       ROFFSUB_STYLE, ROFFSUB_SGR, and ROFFSUB_MEASURE override the style, the
       emphasis parameters, and the measure; TERM=dumb selects plain.
       ROFFSUB_HEADING moves section headings that many columns in from the
       margin, leaving everything else in place.

   UUssaaggee
       --hh serves a different purpose from the manual: a quick reference, a
       refresher and confirmation when a full review is not needed. It is
       composed for that purpose, not extracted from the manual.

   PPrrooffiilleess
       A man page is built from the source and installed in every case. Inside
       the program the manual is carried in one of three profiles.

        PPRROOFFIILLEE        TTHHEE PPRROOGGRRAAMM CCAARRRRIIEESS           HHEELLPP GGIIVVEESS
       --------------+-----------------------------+----------------------------
        full           records and the renderer      any width, emphasis by
                                                     terminal
        preformatted   text set at 80 columns,       fixed width, emphasis by
                       fonts kept, and the emitter   terminal
        plain          text set at 80 columns        fixed width

       The full profile is used when the renderer is no larger than the program
       it would live in, not counting the manual, and an exported bash function
       stays within its size budget; otherwise preformatted. Plain is used only
       when a program asks for it.

CCOOMMPPIILLEEDD FFOORRMM
       One record per line: an operation, then fields separated by tabs. The
       operations are th, sh, ss, pp, tp, ip, rs, re, nf, fi, tx, sp, br, and
       for tables ts, tf, tr, t_, and te; the first record is v 1. Text fields
       carry only \fB, \fI, \fR, \\, and \~, and end in roman. Records are
       passed to awk on its standard input, never with -v, which would rewrite
       their backslashes.

CCOONNFFOORRMMAANNCCEE
        IIDD    RRUULLEE
       -----+---------------------------------------------------------
        E01   byte outside 0x20 to 0x7E
        E02   request not in roffsub, or an apostrophe control line
        E03   escape not in roffsub
        E04   unbalanced .nf/.fi, .RS/.RE, .TS/.TE, or T{/T}
        E05   fill-mode text line empty or beginning with a space
        E06   font not roman at a request line or cell end
        E07   the table line and the tables disagree
        E08   table form
        E09   .TH missing or not the first request
        E10   request arguments
        E11   .TP without a tag line
        W01   option hyphen written without \-
        W02   abbreviation ending a line without \&
        W03   table wider than 71 columns with no text blocks to wrap

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
       Each example shows its source, then the same source rendered in place.

   OOppeenn ttaabbllee wwiitthh aa hheeaaddeerr rruullee
       .TS
       tab(@);
       lb lb
       l  l.
       OPTION@MEANING
       _
       \fB\-r\fR@reverse the order
       \fB\-k\fR \fIkey\fR@sort on \fIkey\fR
       .TE

        OOPPTTIIOONN   MMEEAANNIINNGG
       --------+-------------------
        --rr       reverse the order
        --kk _k_e_y   sort on _k_e_y

   BBooxxeedd ttaabbllee,, aalliiggnneedd ccoolluummnnss
       The box option adds rules above and below; keys r and c align right and
       center.

       .TS
       box tab(@);
       lb cb rb
       l  c  r.
       NAME@KIND@SIZE
       _
       alpha@file@12
       beta@directory@3456
       .TE

       -------+-----------+------
        NNAAMMEE      KKIINNDD      SSIIZZEE
       -------+-----------+------
        alpha     file        12
        beta    directory   3456
       -------+-----------+------

   RRuulleedd ttaabbllee,, cceenntteerreedd
       The allbox option puts a rule after every row; center places the table in
       the middle of the line.

       .TS
       allbox center tab(@);
       cb cb
       r  l.
       CODE@MEANING
       0@success
       1@a check found errors
       2@a usage or input error
       .TE

                            ------+------------------------
                             CCOODDEE          MMEEAANNIINNGG
                            ------+------------------------
                                0   success
                            ------+------------------------
                                1   a check found errors
                            ------+------------------------
                                2   a usage or input error
                            ------+------------------------

   TTeexxtt bblloocckkss tthhaatt wwrraapp
       A cell written between T{ and T} wraps within its column, in roff and in
       the roffsub renderers alike.

       .TS
       tab(@);
       lb lb
       l  l.
       TERM@DEFINITION
       _
       measure@T{
       the longest line that filled text may reach,
       whatever the width of the terminal
       T}
       prevailing indent@T{
       the indent that tagged and indented paragraphs use
       when they give none of their own
       T}
       .TE

        TTEERRMM                DDEEFFIINNIITTIIOONN
       -------------------+-----------------------------------------------------
        measure             the longest line that filled text may reach,
                            whatever the width of the terminal
        prevailing indent   the indent that tagged and indented paragraphs use
                            when they give none of their own

   MMiinniimmuumm ccoolluummnn wwiiddtthh
       A key with w(N) keeps its column at least N cells wide.

       .TS
       tab(@);
       lw(12) l.
       key@value
       .TE

        key            value

   DDeeffiinniittiioonnss wwiitthh aa nnaarrrrooww iinnddeenntt
       A width on .TP sets the prevailing indent for the items that follow.

       .TP 4
       .B a
       first
       .TP
       .B b
       second

       aa   first

       bb   second

   NNuummbbeerreedd lliisstt aanndd nneesstteedd bblloocckk
       .IP 1. 4
       Write the page.
       .IP 2.
       Check it:
       .RS
       .nf
       roffsub \-c page.1
       .fi
       .RE

       1.  Write the page.

       2.  Check it:
           roffsub -c page.1

   EEmmpphhaassiiss
       .BR roffsub (1)
       reads \fIfile\fR with \fB\-w\fR \fIwidth\fR.

       rrooffffssuubb(1) reads _f_i_l_e with --ww _w_i_d_t_h.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
       rrooffffssuubb(1), mmaann(7), ttbbll(1)
